  <h1>Standard vs Advanced Techniques</h1>
  <p>
    In histopathology, traditional blade cutting and modern laser microdissection represent two very different approaches to tissue preparation. 
    Below is a comparison highlighting their differences and the advantages of advanced laser technology.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr>
      <th>Feature</th>
      <th>Blade Cutting (Traditional)</th>
      <th>Laser Microdissection (Advanced)</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Method</td>
      <td>Steel/diamond blade slices tissue mechanically</td>
      <td>Focused laser beam isolates cells/tissue without contact</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Precision</td>
      <td>Limited by blade thickness and vibration</td>
      <td>Micrometer-level precision, ideal for single cells</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Contamination Risk</td>
      <td>Possible due to blade wear and debris</td>
      <td>Minimal, since no physical contact occurs</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Tissue Damage</td>
      <td>Mechanical stress can distort or crack tissue</td>
      <td>Minimal mechanical stress, morphology preserved</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Speed & Efficiency</td>
      <td>Manual, slower, operator-dependent</td>
      <td>Automated targeting, faster and reproducible</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Biosafety</td>
      <td>Risk of contamination from blades</td>
      <td>Enhanced biosafety with sterile, contact-free cutting</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Key Advantages of Advanced Techniques</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Faster sample preparation</li>
    <li>Standardized and reproducible results</li>
    <li>Reduced human error</li>
    <li>Improved biosafety and accuracy</li>
  </ul>
